---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base title="Decompression - FastQC">
  <p class="label">Decompression</p>
  <h1>Parallel gzip decompression</h1>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    <h2>Why gzip is single-threaded</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      Gzip is built to be read from front to back. A DEFLATE stream packs data into
      variable-length codes with no byte alignment and no separators between its blocks,
      so a decoder cannot find where one block ends without first decoding the block
      before it. Each block can also copy runs of bytes from up to 32&nbsp;KB earlier in
      the decompressed output, so block N depends on the output of block N&minus;1.
      Standard tools like <code>gzip</code> and zlib follow that dependency and decode on
      a single core, which is why decompression is usually the slowest part of reading a
      large <code>.fastq.gz</code>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    <h2>Decoding chunks in parallel</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted); margin-bottom: 0.75rem">
      FastQC-Rust decodes gzip with
      <a href="https://crates.io/crates/rapidgzip-core">rapidgzip-core</a>, which breaks
      that dependency by decoding chunks of the file at once, before it knows what precedes
      them. A worker jumps partway into the stream and treats every back-reference into the
      still-unknown history as a placeholder marker rather than a real byte. The expensive
      work, Huffman decoding and expanding the copied runs, then runs in parallel across
      chunks. A second, cheap pass replaces the markers with real bytes once the preceding
      window is available. The decode scales with the number of cores; the fix-up is a table
      lookup and a memory copy.
    </p>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      Starting a chunk means finding a point that looks like the beginning of a DEFLATE
      block, which rapidgzip does by scanning for a valid block header and confirming it by
      decoding and checking it against its neighbours. A bad guess falls back to ordinary
      sequential decoding. Files that are already written in independent blocks, such as
      BGZF from <code>bgzip</code> or multi-member output from <code>pigz</code>, need no
      guessing: rapidgzip splits on the block boundaries and decodes them in parallel
      directly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    <h2>A faster core, and overlap</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      The inflate itself uses <a href="https://crates.io/crates/zlib-rs">zlib-rs</a>, a
      modern implementation with SIMD paths that beats stock zlib even on a single thread.
      Because the decoder also works on its own threads, decompression overlaps with
      FastQC's per-record analysis instead of alternating with it, so both make progress at
      the same time. Peak memory stays bounded regardless of file size, because the decoder
      only keeps a small window of decoded chunks in flight while the parser consumes them.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem">
    <h2>What this means in practice</h2>
    <p style="color: var(--ink-muted)">
      On a four-core machine this cuts the wall-clock time of a large gzipped FASTQ by
      roughly 1.5x end to end, and decompression measured on its own runs several times
      faster. The ceiling comes from the rest of FastQC: its analysis modules run on one
      thread and handle reads in order, so once decompression stops being the bottleneck,
      that serial work sets the pace. With more cores, the decompression speedup grows and
      the analysis becomes the next thing worth optimising. The parallel-decoding approach
      comes from the <a href="https://github.com/mxmlnkn/rapidgzip">rapidgzip</a> project
      and its predecessor <a href="https://github.com/Piezoid/pugz">pugz</a>, both built for
      genomic data, and produces output byte-for-byte identical to a serial decoder.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
